.TH EJSON-KEYGEN 1 "January 2026" "ejson" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ejson-keygen \- generate an EJSON keypair for encrypting secrets
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ejson keygen
[\fB\-k\fR|\fB\-\-keydir\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-w\fR|\fB\-\-write\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ejson keygen
generates a new public/private keypair for use with EJSON secret encryption.
The keypair uses public-key, elliptic curve cryptography (NaCl Box:
Curve25519 + Salsa20 + Poly1305-AES).
.PP
By default, both the public and private keys are printed to stdout. This is
useful if you need to distribute the private key to multiple servers via
configuration management or other secure channels.
.PP
When called with the
.B \-w
option, the private key is written to the keydir (a file named after the
public key), and only the public key is printed to stdout. This is the
recommended workflow for most use cases.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \fB\-\-keydir\fR=\fIpath\fR
Path to directory containing private keys. Defaults to
.IR /opt/ejson/keys .
Setting
.B EJSON_KEYDIR
will also set this value, with lower precedence.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-write
Write the private key to a file in the keydir, named after the public key.
Only the public key will be printed to stdout. The key file is created with
restrictive permissions (0440 on Unix systems) to prevent unauthorized access.
.SH OUTPUT
Without
.BR \-w :
.PP
.RS
.nf
WARNING: The private key will be displayed below.
         This key should be kept secret and stored securely.
         Consider using 'ejson keygen -w' to write directly to keydir.
         Terminal scrollback may retain this key.

Public Key:
63ccf05a9492e68e12eeb1c705888aebdcc0080af7e594fc402beb24cce9d14f
Private Key:
75b80b4a693156eb435f4ed2fe397e583f461f09fd99ec2bd1bdef0a56cf6e64
.fi
.RE
.PP
With
.BR \-w :
.PP
.RS
.nf
53393332c6c7c474af603c078f5696c8fe16677a09a711bba299a6c1c1676a59
.fi
.RE
.PP
The private key is written to
.IR /opt/ejson/keys/53393332c6c7c474af603c078f5696c8fe16677a09a711bba299a6c1c1676a59 .
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Generate and display both keys (with security warning):
.RS
.nf
$ ejson keygen
WARNING: The private key will be displayed below.
         This key should be kept secret and stored securely.
         Consider using 'ejson keygen -w' to write directly to keydir.
         Terminal scrollback may retain this key.

Public Key:
63ccf05a9492e68e12eeb1c705888aebdcc0080af7e594fc402beb24cce9d14f
Private Key:
75b80b4a693156eb435f4ed2fe397e583f461f09fd99ec2bd1bdef0a56cf6e64
.fi
.RE
.PP
Generate keypair and write private key to default keydir (recommended):
.RS
.nf
$ ejson keygen -w
53393332c6c7c474af603c078f5696c8fe16677a09a711bba299a6c1c1676a59
.fi
.RE
.PP
Generate keypair and write to a custom keydir:
.RS
.nf
$ ejson keygen -w --keydir /path/to/custom/keys
a1b2c3d4e5f6...
.fi
.RE
.PP
View the stored private key:
.RS
.nf
$ cat /opt/ejson/keys/53393332c6c7c474af603c078f5696c8fe16677a09a711bba299a6c1c1676a59
888a4291bef9135729357b8c70e5a62b0bbe104a679d829cdbe56d46a4481aaf
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use the public key in a secrets file:
.RS
.nf
$ ejson keygen -w > /tmp/pubkey.txt
$ cat secrets.ejson
{
  "_public_key": "$(cat /tmp/pubkey.txt)",
  "database_password": "supersecret123"
}
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (key generation failed, failed to create keydir,
failed to write key file, etc.).
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
When using
.B ejson keygen
without
.BR \-w ,
the private key is displayed in the terminal. This may be retained in
terminal scrollback, shell history, or screen recordings. Use
.B \-w
whenever possible.
.IP \(bu 2
Private key files are created with permissions 0440 (owner and group read-only)
on Unix systems to prevent unauthorized access or accidental modification.
.IP \(bu 2
The private key is automatically cleared from memory after use (zeroized)
to prevent memory disclosure attacks.
.IP \(bu 2
The keydir should be protected with appropriate filesystem permissions.
Only systems that need to decrypt secrets should have access to private keys.
.IP \(bu 2
Each secrets file should use a unique keypair. Do not reuse keypairs across
unrelated projects.
.IP \(bu 2
On non-Unix platforms (e.g., Windows), restrictive file permissions are not
automatically applied. Take care to manually secure key files on these systems.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EJSON_KEYDIR
Directory containing private keys. Defaults to
.IR /opt/ejson/keys .
Can be overridden by the
.B \-\-keydir
option.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /opt/ejson/keys
Default directory for storing private keys. Each key file is named after the
64-byte hex-encoded public key and contains the corresponding private key.
.SH CRYPTOGRAPHY
EJSON uses NaCl Box for public-key authenticated encryption:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Curve25519
\- Elliptic curve Diffie-Hellman key agreement
.IP \(bu 2
.B Salsa20
\- Stream cipher for symmetric encryption
.IP \(bu 2
.B Poly1305-AES
\- Message authentication code
.PP
Keys are 256-bit (32 bytes) and represented as 64-character hexadecimal strings.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ejson (1),
.BR ejson (5),
.BR ejson\-encrypt (1),
.BR ejson\-decrypt (1),
.BR ejson\-env (1)
